==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|fulkan||people|lang=gem-pro}}, from ''*fulka-'' ‘crowd, army’.  Cognate with Old Frisian [[folk]], Old Saxon ''folc'', Old Dutch ''folk'' (Dutch [[volk]]), Old High German ''folc'' (German [[Volk]]), Old Norse [[folk]] ‘people, army’. Possibly ultimately from a {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|pl̥h₁-go|lang=ine-pro}}. Thought by some to be related to Latin ''[[vulgar]]/[[vulgus]]'', but this is uncertain.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/folk/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|g=n}}

# [[people]], [[nation]], [[race]]
# [[army]]
# {{in the plural|lang=ang}} people in general

